      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the weight of one bowling ball, we can start by defining the variables for the weight of the bowling balls and the canoes. Let's denote the weight of one bowling ball as \\( B \\) and the weight of one canoe as \\( C \\).\n\nAccording to the problem, seven bowling balls weigh the same as three canoes. This can be written as:\n\\[ 7B = 3C \\]\n\nWe are also given that two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds. This can be written as:\n\\[ 2C = 56 \\]\n\nFirst, we solve for \\( C \\) by dividing both sides of the equation \\( 2C = 56 \\) by 2:\n\\[ C = \\frac{56}{2} = 28 \\]\n\nNow that we know the weight of one canoe is 28 pounds, we substitute \\( C = 28 \\) into the equation \\( 7B = 3C \\):\n\\[ 7B = 3 \\times 28 \\]\n\\[ 7B = 84 \\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( B \\) by dividing both sides of the equation \\( 7B = 84 \\) by 7:\n\\[ B = \\frac{84}{7} = 12 \\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
