    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the relationships:\n1. Seven identical bowling balls weigh the same as three identical canoes.\n2. Two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds.\n\nFirst, determine the total weight of two canoes:\n\\[\n2 \\text{ canoes} = 56 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\nTherefore, the weight of one canoe is:\n\\[\n\\frac{56 \\text{ pounds}}{2} = 28 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the information that seven bowling balls weigh the same as three canoes. Let \\( B \\) represent the weight of one bowling ball, and \\( C \\) represent the weight of one canoe. According to the problem, we have:\n\\[\n7B = 3C\n\\]\n\nWe know the weight of one canoe \\( C \\) is 28 pounds, so substitute \\( C = 28 \\) into the equation:\n\\[\n7B = 3 \\times 28\n\\]\n\\[\n7B = 84\n\\]\n\nTo find the weight of one bowling ball \\( B \\), solve for \\( B \\):\n\\[\nB = \\frac{84}{7} = 12\n\\]\n\nThus, the weight of one bowling ball is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
