      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to set up a system of equations based on the given information. Let's define the variables first:\n\n- Let \\( B \\) be the weight of one bowling ball in pounds.\n- Let \\( C \\) be the weight of one canoe in pounds.\n\nFrom the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n1. Seven bowling balls weigh the same as three canoes.\n2. Two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds.\n\nWe can translate these pieces of information into the following equations:\n1. \\( 7B = 3C \\)\n2. \\( 2C = 56 \\)\n\nFirst, we solve the second equation for \\( C \\):\n\\[ 2C = 56 \\]\n\\[ C = \\frac{56}{2} \\]\n\\[ C = 28 \\]\n\nNow that we know the weight of one canoe \\( C \\) is 28 pounds, we can substitute this value into the first equation to find the weight of one bowling ball \\( B \\):\n\\[ 7B = 3C \\]\n\\[ 7B = 3 \\times 28 \\]\n\\[ 7B = 84 \\]\n\\[ B = \\frac{84}{7} \\]\n\\[ B = 12 \\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
